<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuno da Cunha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1487">1487</a>–<a href="/years/1539">1539</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nuno da Cunha was a Portuguese governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1529 to 1538. On his way to India in 1528 he attacked and burned Mombasa, and in office he secured Diu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, where the Portuguese built a fortress in 1535 by agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Bahadur Shah, and extended Portuguese power at Bassein on the Indian coast. He died at sea on the voyage home.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuno-da-cunha.jpg" alt="Nuno da Cunha" width="320" height="593" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuno_da_Cunha_(Archaeological_Survey_of_India,_Goa).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born about 1487, Nuno da Cunha was the son of Antónia Pais and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tristao-da-cunha">Tristão da Cunha</a>, the navigator, admiral and ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a>. He first proved himself in the fighting at Oja and Brava and at the capture of Panane, under the viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a>. King John III of Portugal, to whom he had been close since the king was a prince, named him ninth governor of the Portuguese possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with orders to end the government of Lopo Vaz de Sampaio and send him to Portugal in chains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> he subdued at Mombasa on the passage out had been harassing the coast of Portuguese Mozambique, whose watering stations were essential to the route to western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. His brothers Pero Vaz da Cunha and Simão da Cunha were to have served as his second and third in command, as was expected in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-da-india">Estado da Índia</a>, but both died on the voyage, and during his long rule he had to rely on local networks of clientage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>. He took office in April 1529 and held it for nine years, longer than any other governor of Portuguese Asia in the sixteenth century.</p><p>In 1529 he sent an expedition that sacked and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daman">Damão</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. On 20 January 1533 forces under his control captured Baxay, the later Bassein and present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasai">Vasai</a>, from Bahadur Shah. The next year it became the capital of the Portuguese province of the North, and its citadel of black basalt was begun; the citadel was completed in 1548 and still stands. In 1538 an army of the Sultanate of Gujarat under Khadjar Safar, aided by Ottoman forces, besieged the Portuguese in Diu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Court intrigues forced his return to Portugal. He was shipwrecked at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> on the way and drowned; his death is dated 5 March 1539. He had married first Maria da Cunha and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-isabel-island">Isabel</a> da Silveira. The main source for his career is the history of the Portuguese in the East by João de Barros, who lived from 1496 to 1570.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nuno da Cunha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The town recovered and remained openly hostile, and in 1528 a Portuguese fleet under <b>Nuno da Cunha</b> attacked and burned it again.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-34.36,18.47&amp;year=1520" aria-label="Cape of Good Hope on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5020 3408 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Nuno da Cunha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5470 3678h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5470 3678h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-34.36,18.47&amp;year=1520">What was here in 1520? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
